      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "French explorers called the body of water Lac Tipiconeau, meaning buffalo fish, a name that evolved into today’s Fish Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish_Lake%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Upper Fish Lake spans roughly 133 acres, reaches a depth of 23 feet, and is fed by Mill Creek and Fish Creek before draining southward.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fish_lake_indiana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1921 Dispatcher’s Cabin, built by the first fire crew, now stands as a preserved historic building in Fish Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
